1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,100
A business brief looks simple: a logo, customer and supplier details, line items, totals, payment terms.

2
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:12,357
The hard part is turning it into a Word document that survives the real file.

3
00:00:12,357 --> 00:00:14,882
The logo must stay on the first page.

4
00:00:14,882 --> 00:00:19,115
Later pages need the company identity, but not the logo again.

5
00:00:19,115 --> 00:00:22,392
And every number must come from the right field.

6
00:00:22,392 --> 00:00:25,352
Here is the same brief in the live demo.

7
00:00:25,352 --> 00:00:31,198
The data source is a DDSP package: typed fields, collections and sample values.

8
00:00:31,198 --> 00:00:34,528
The planner is not allowed to invent a field.

9
00:00:34,528 --> 00:00:37,488
It can only choose from schema evidence.

10
00:00:37,488 --> 00:00:39,668
The schema is the source of truth.

11
00:00:39,668 --> 00:00:45,246
Fields have types, collections have scope, and sample data shows how values are shaped.

12
00:00:45,246 --> 00:00:51,017
The planner can search those candidates, but the resolver decides whether a path is legal.

13
00:00:51,017 --> 00:00:55,056
That is why a confident sentence cannot become a wrong binding.

14
00:00:55,056 --> 00:00:59,354
Generate the document and the brief becomes concrete requirements.

15
00:00:59,354 --> 00:01:04,434
Each requirement points to a candidate field, with its type and scope visible.

16
00:01:04,434 --> 00:01:08,472
That is the difference between a prompt and a reviewable plan.

17
00:01:08,472 --> 00:01:13,867
The mapping review separates confirmed bindings from ambiguous or missing ones.

18
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:18,579
When a choice is ambiguous, a reviewer can confirm the correct field.

19
00:01:18,579 --> 00:01:23,155
The decision is recorded, and a blocking mapping stops compilation.

20
00:01:23,155 --> 00:01:25,272
Nothing silently falls through.

21
00:01:25,272 --> 00:01:32,744
The preview and the Word file are built from the same template structure, so headers and layout follow the same rules.

22
00:01:32,744 --> 00:01:35,847
When the mapping is ready, download the document.

23
00:01:35,847 --> 00:01:39,456
The demo also records the artifact hash for verification.

24
00:01:39,456 --> 00:01:41,780
The demo does not stop at pixels.

25
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:45,301
Downloading the file records its size and SHA-256.

26
00:01:45,301 --> 00:01:55,512
The same hash is reproduced by the deterministic local build, so a reviewer can verify that the demo and the evidence describe the same artifact.

27
00:01:55,512 --> 00:01:57,220
Open the result in Word.

28
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:00,067
The logo appears only on the first page.

29
00:02:00,067 --> 00:02:07,968
The invoice number, customer and supplier details, items and totals are all there, and the table stays aligned.

30
00:02:07,968 --> 00:02:13,596
Page two keeps the company name and VAT in the header, without repeating the logo.

31
00:02:13,596 --> 00:02:15,929
The footer keeps its page numbers.

32
00:02:15,929 --> 00:02:20,184
That is the first-page header rule working in a real document.

33
00:02:20,184 --> 00:02:26,791
The brief became a typed plan, the plan became a validated document, and every binding has evidence.

34
00:02:26,791 --> 00:02:31,152
The same pipeline covers quotations, invoices and purchase orders.

35
00:02:31,152 --> 00:02:36,504
Docentric Copilot is a narrow AI boundary around a deterministic document engine.
